-- title:
--   Drinfeld chart at a supersingular point with level and inertia riders
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ and a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$ (`hqM'`), and a prime $\ell$ with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ (`hℓ12`) and $\ell \mid M'$ (`hℓM'`).
--
--   **Constant field data.** $L$ is a field of characteristic zero, $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hζ`), $\xi \in L$ is a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity (`hξ`), these are pinned together by $\zeta = \xi^{\ell}$ (`hζξ`), and `hι` asserts the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\xi) = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$.
--
--   **Level data.** $H_1$ is a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^{\times}$ required by `hH₁` to equal [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22) intersected with the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^{\times} \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^{\times}$; here `levelH q M'` is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^{\times} \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^{\times}$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$. The intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ is required by `hK` to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103): the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise map $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q} \to \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$, of the rational $q$-expansion function field attached to the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ (the subgroup of $\Gamma_0(q^2M')$ cut out by $H_1$).
--
--   **Base ring data.** $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with $L$ as fraction field, with algebraically closed residue field, such that $q$ lies in the maximal ideal (`hAq`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$ (`hζA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j \in K$ is required by `hj` to have Laurent series the image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (the rational $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-invariant) under the coefficient embedding, and $j \neq 0$. The element $\varpi \in A$ generates the maximal ideal (`hϖ`), and $t \in A$ satisfies $t^{q-1} = q\,w$ for some unit $w$ (`ht`).
--
--   **Point data.** Write $X =$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $A$-algebras of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$, respectively over $A[j^{-1}]$. The point $z$ of $X$ lies over the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} A$: the element $\varpi z$ of the stalk of $X$ at $z$ is by `hϖz` the germ at $z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under the structure morphism $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, and `hz` requires $\varpi z$ to lie in the maximal ideal of that stalk. The point $y$ of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of `chartAlgFin A K j` satisfies $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}(y) = z$ (`hy`).
--
--   **Supersingularity.** The hypothesis `hss` requires: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the finite chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel the prime $y$, the image $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), that is, every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $\varphi(j)$ has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist a complete discrete valuation domain $W$ (a domain which is a discrete valuation ring and is adically complete for its maximal ideal), a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u$ and $v$ units and
--   $$f - (X_0X_1^{q} - X_0^{q}X_1) \in (X_0, X_1)^{q+2},$$
--   and a ring isomorphism
--   $$e : \widehat{\mathcal{O}_{X,z}} \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(\sigma t)\,v - f\,u\bigr),$$
--   where $\widehat{\mathcal{O}_{X,z}}$ is the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ with respect to its maximal ideal, such that the following three assertions hold. Throughout, `toC` denotes the canonical map from the stalk to its completion, `mkS` the quotient map onto $S$, and `germY` the canonical ring map from the finite chart algebra to the stalk at $z$ (the germ at $z$ on the image open of $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}$, precomposed with the canonical identifications of sections with the chart algebra).
--
--   (i) *Constants.* For every $a \in A$, the image under $e$ of the germ at $z$ of $a$ (pulled back along $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and pushed into the completion) is the class of the constant $C(\sigma a)$.
--
--   (ii) *Level automorphisms.* For every $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lying in $\Gamma_0(M')$, every $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ with [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — that is: for all $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, all modular forms $f_1, g_1$ of weight $k$ for $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f, p_g$, $p_g$ nonzero as a Laurent series, and all $x \in K$ whose Laurent series is the image of $p_f/p_g$, and every $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = \exp(2\pi i/q)$, one has $\iota(\tau x) \cdot (g_1 \mid_k c_q(\gamma^{-1}))^{\wedge} = (f_1 \mid_k c_q(\gamma^{-1}))^{\wedge}$ as $q$-expansions, where $c_q(\delta)$ is the matrix $\begin{pmatrix} \delta_{00} & \delta_{01}/q \\ q\,\delta_{10} & \delta_{11}\end{pmatrix}$ — and every proof `hpres` that $\tau$ maps the finite chart algebra into itself, if the restriction $\tau'$ of $\tau$ to that algebra satisfies $\tau'(a) - a \in y$ for all $a$, then there exist a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in \mathrm{Mat}_2(W)$ such that:
--
--   (ii.a) $\theta$ transports $\tau$: $\theta(e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(a)))) = e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\tau'(a))))$ for all $a$ in the finite chart algebra;
--
--   (ii.b) $\theta$ fixes the constants: $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}(C(w))) = \mathrm{mkS}(C(w))$ for all $w \in W$;
--
--   (ii.c) $M$ is the linear part of $\theta$: for each $j' \in \{0,1\}$, $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}(X_{j'})) - \mathrm{mkS}\bigl(\sum_{i} C(M_{ij'})X_i\bigr)$ lies in the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0, X_1$;
--
--   (ii.d) $c^{q+1} \equiv 1 \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_W}$;
--
--   (ii.e) $M_{ij'} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij'} \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_W}$ for all $i, j'$;
--
--   (ii.f) if $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$ then $c \equiv 1 \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_W}$;
--
--   (ii.g) if $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity on $K$, then $c \not\equiv 1 \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_W}$.
--
--   (iii) *Inertia.* For every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/q)^{\times}$, every ring automorphism $\sigma_L$ of $L$ and every ring automorphism $\sigma_A$ of $A$ compatible with $\sigma_L$ along $A \to L$, with $\sigma_A(a) - a \in \mathfrak{m}_A$ for all $a$; for every $\pi \in A$ with $\pi^{q^2-1} = q$ and every $\alpha \in A$ with $\sigma_A(\pi) = \alpha\pi$ such that $\alpha^{q+1} \equiv \widetilde{d} \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_A}$, where $\widetilde{d}$ is the image in $A$ of the natural-number representative of $d$; and for every ring automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ acting on Laurent series coefficientwise through $\sigma_L$ (i.e. the Laurent series of $\tau x$ is [`ModularCurve.coeffMap σL`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16) applied to that of $x$): then $\tau$ maps the finite chart algebra into itself, and for every proof `hpres` of that stability, first $\tau'(a) - a \in y$ for all $a$ in the finite chart algebra, and second there exist a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, a ring automorphism $\sigma_W$ of $W$, an element $c_t \in W$ and a matrix $M \in \mathrm{Mat}_2(W)$ such that:
--
--   (iii.a) $\theta(e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(a)))) = e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\tau'(a))))$ for all $a$ in the finite chart algebra;
--
--   (iii.b) $\sigma_W \circ \sigma = \sigma \circ \sigma_A$ on $A$;
--
--   (iii.c) $\sigma_W(w) - w \in \mathfrak{m}_W$ for all $w \in W$;
--
--   (iii.d) $\theta$ is $\sigma_W$-semilinear on constants: $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}(C(w))) = \mathrm{mkS}(C(\sigma_W w))$ for all $w \in W$;
--
--   (iii.e) $M$ is the linear part of $\theta$, in the same sense as (ii.c);
--
--   (iii.f) $c_t \equiv \sigma(\alpha^{q+1}) \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_W}$;
--
--   (iii.g) for all $i, j'$, $M_{ij'} \equiv c_t \cdot \bigl(\mathrm{diagOneElem}\,q\,(d^{q})^{-1}\bigr)_{ij'} \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_W}$, where the entries of the matrix $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & (d^{q})^{-1}\end{pmatrix}$ over $\mathbb{Z}/q$ are lifted to $W$ via their natural-number representatives.
--
--   This is the local-structure statement for the two-chart integral model of the modular curve of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ at a supersingular point of its fibre over $q$: the completed local ring is presented as a Drinfeld-type chart $W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma t)v - fu)$ with $f$ congruent to $X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and the presentation is made equivariant both for the automorphisms coming from the level structure (with linear part $c\gamma$ and the rider distinguishing the diamond at $\ell$) and for the inertia action (with linear part $c_t\,\mathrm{diag}(1,(d^q)^{-1})$). The version here carries no lower bound on $q$, the auxiliary level being handled by the guard prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ dividing $M'$, and it is used in the computation of the tame character on the linear part of inertia in terms of the diamond operator.
